  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own. The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" The two reply texts are, in full: first, `{\"a\":1} and then \"{\"b\":2}\"`; second, `{\"b\":2}`. For the first reply, the only top-level JSON object is `{\"a\":1}`; the `{\"b\":2}` appears inside a double-quoted string and is therefore not a top-level object, so the rule requires the answer `{\"a\":1}`. For the second reply, the top-level object is `{\"b\":2}`, so the rule requires `{\"b\":2}`. I conjecture that the function returns `{\"b\":2}` for both replies, because its top-level scanner uses `raw_decode` at every `{` character without tracking whether that `{` is inside a string, so it treats the string-embedded `{\"b\":2}` as a top-level candidate and, since it is the last candidate, returns it. This would make the check give the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if running the function on the first reply returns `{\"a\":1}` or raises an error instead of returning `{\"b\":2}`.",
